WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.160
My name is Daniel Goldman I'm associate
professor of physics at Georgia Tech and

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.589
my group is interested in how complex
behavior can emerge from the

00:00:09.589 --> 00:00:16.100
interactions of many simple agents or
elements working together and in our

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.720
group we're taking what we call a robo
physics approach where robotics meets

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.670
physics thinking and we're creating very
simple but controllable little robots

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.470
smart active particles or smarticles
as we call them and studying how changes

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:36.440
in their interaction properties lead to
group level behaviors we find that in

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.780
fact if we shine a light on some of
these smarticles each happens to be

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:48.079
light-sensitive we can actually steer
the collective without the ability for

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.190
any one of the smarticles to move
on its own one of the problems we're

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:58.130
interested in is how a group of smarticles neither none of which can move on

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.850
its own can actually come together to
form an entity which can move across a

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.870
table so what are the rules and what are
the programs one would write to tell a

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.500
group of smarticles to come together
to move across the table or to come

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.140
together to bunch up this is really an
intersection and a new intersection of

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.780
physics active matter physics and
computer science

